A||||16||Gold87SE||Headlight Motors||||1||
Z||000000||Gold87SE||08-09-2000||02:49 AM||||Is there any way that I can repair my headlight motors?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||08-09-2000||03:00 AM||||Yes, If the gear is stripped open it up and turn it over. The Fiero headlight motors only use 1/2 the gear. If the bushings are worn I think Rodney has replacements. There also use to be a place that sold brass gears but I don't remember who or where.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jmw||08-09-2000||02:03 PM||||I was trying to get into one of my headlight motors last night. Found a diagram on a website, but all it really said was open it up, clean it, replace the grease, and replace two little curvy rubber-looking doodads (are these the bushings?). My questions are (1) how do you get the pegs out that hold the two sides of the motor together???  They look like screws, but have no screw-holes. I tried a nail / hammer from the flip-side, but that didn't work. And (2) what kind of grease do you use? [Sorry, I'm a novice]<P>Usedfieroparts.com advertises motors for $35. I can't get them to email me back, but want to try to fix myself first anyway. <P>Also, there was a post on 7-30 called "headlight motors - I need" that suggests a glue-stick fix. You may have seen that already.<p>[This message has been edited by jmw (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||08-10-2000||01:05 PM||||Those are rivets. To get them out, support the motor flange that they go through on a block of wood and very carefully drill on the open end of the rivet just enough to remove the portion that has been peened over. It isn't too bad if you get the hole a little large - just don't break off the plastic that it goes through. <P>Rodney Dickman sells a kit with two gears, two harnesses, screws for two motors, two packets of silicone grease, screws and nuts to replace the rivets in both motors and instructions for $85.00.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 08-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||jmw||08-10-2000||08:44 PM||||Thanks. Will get drill and try that. Also found site called Fiero Online Service that has trouble-shooting for a wide variety of problems. Don't know how good it is, or how up-to-date it is, but it is at...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.qtm.net/~lcur/osg.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.qtm.net/~lcur/osg.htm</A> <P>You have to scroll down to the individual topics -- the headings at the top of the site appear to be broken links.||||reg||7
Z||000005||overlord||08-10-2000||09:23 PM||||i recelntly had a motor go on me...so i drilled out the rivets, opened it up and found that the big plastic gear is stripped in one spot.<P>so i can just flip that gear over and it should work fine? (if it only uses half on the gear)||||reg||1
Z||000006||chris6878||08-10-2000||11:19 PM||||My headlight motor was continually running in both directions..  up and down..  i replaced the plastic gear... but it still does the same,  goes up and down...   but still runs...      what else could i check?||||reg||3
Z||000007||Gold87SE||08-11-2000||02:49 AM||||From what that page says, the 87-88 headlight motors are designed so the bushings will break rather than the gears stripping. That means that flipping the gears won't work for me, is this correct?||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||08-11-2000||08:32 AM||||The nylon gear is the one that strips - also the green rubber bumpers in the gear hub disintegrate with age. I'd say take your motor apart and look at the gear. If half of the gear is not stripped (and the green rubber bumpers are ok) then turn it over and re-assemble after cleaning out the old grease and relubing the metal gears.||||reg||1
Z||000009||overlord||08-11-2000||08:46 AM||||hmmm.....i turned around the plastic gear (it was only stripped in one spot the size of the metal gear), and the light goes up and down......but teh same problem as chris6878 is happening.....the motor wont shut off when its supposed to.<P>How exactly does the motor know when to stop?<BR>84 motor.||||reg||1
Z||000010||William Federle||08-11-2000||01:15 PM||||In the haynes manual there's a page in the back that shows the headlight motor circuit. There's two switches that are activated when the mechanism reaches its end of travel. Since it can't move any more, the motion of the worm gear causes it to travel up or down<BR>lengthwise (I think axially might be the word). This motion activates a switch that disconnects power from the motor. The switch<BR>(actually two of them) is part of the brush assembly which is installed or removed through the opening covered by a flat plate on the side of the motor. They are part of the kit I mentioned previously. If the motor doesn't shut off, I think it might be because that switch isn't activating. Maybe it's broken or maybe the gear being stripped prevents the torque from building up and forcing the worm-shaft to activate the switch.||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||08-11-2000||03:04 PM||||In essence, the headlight motor stops when it "feels"  a physical stop - in other words, when the headlight-raising linkage hits the limit of its travel.  <P>In the case of the early design (1984-86) motors, the high torque switch (HTS) feels the resistance of the linkage bottoming out on a stop, and turns the motor off. <P>I find it interesting that it seems a lot of cars with flip-up headlights seem to have problems eventually.  I see numerous Fieros, Firebirds, Probes, Hondas, and others with one or both "eyes" open in the daytime.  In fact, one of my neighbors with a Nissan something-or-other asked me if I knew how to fix his, "Since you work on those little cars with the flip-up headlights."  I had to plead ignorance  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !      <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000012||overlord||08-11-2000||05:27 PM||||HOLY SH**!!<BR>i give up on this stupid headlight motor.<BR>I had it wokring when i was testing it, but when i got everything installed normal...the stupid motor wont even come on to go in the up position.<P>TO THE SHOP!||||reg||1
Z||000013||DaveL||08-12-2000||07:04 PM||||I r4ecently bought an 87 SE that had both headlights held up by a string attached to the metal tube from the a/c accumulator. Was considering drilling a hole through the dash and just pulling the string through the hole. Then I could tie a knot in the string to hold them up, and untie it to lower them. Seriously, I looked around and found I could buy used motors with no guarantee for about $40-$50. After some negotiating with my local GM dealer, I ended up buying a brand new set and replacing the old ones myself. The dealer would have done the whole job for almost $600!!! Bought the new ones for $100 apiece, before tax. When I was done, I took the old ones apart and found that the largish nylon gear was totally stripped, and the compartment was full of the shavings. It occurred to me that if you could just replace the gear, it shouldn't cost more than $5 or so. It never occurred to me that you might be able to just turn them over. Anyway, before that when I turned on the headlight switch, the motors both ran for a few seconds then shut off. You obviously have a problem with either your switch, as mentioned in the previous reply, or the relay.  BTW, you didn't say whether you could raise or lower them manually. If so, the gears are working.||||reg||3
Z||000014||theogre||08-12-2000||07:37 PM||||Gold, others,<P>Correct 87+ motors are diferant. They use plastic pins. No you can't flip the gear. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Rodney Dickman</A> has the pins. 10$us for enough pins for both motors. At that price it's better to just fix the one you have than replace them.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Rodney||08-12-2000||08:21 PM||||When you take apart a 87-88 motor and find all those shavings they are the old coupling pins ground down to small scraps. You will tend to think the gear is stripped but it is really just these shavings. Cheap and easy to rebuild. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||William Federle||08-13-2000||02:17 PM||||I recently saw a picture in a post here in the Fiero Forum of a front facia with external headlights - not a conversion of the existing headlights - these were located on either side of a grill in the front of the facia. It didn't look bad either. You certainly wouldn't have headlight motor problems anymore.<BR>||||reg||1
